 Approaches:

To solve this, we developed and compared two approaches.
The first is the Physics-Based Approach. The workflow is: define the suspension ODE system, simulate road input by solving the ODEs, extract RMS acceleration from the result, and then optimize stiffness and damping using that result as the objective function.
The strengths are significant — it is physically reliable, grounded in first-principles mechanics, requires no training data, and produces fully interpretable results. However, the critical limitation is compute time. Each full optimization run takes 10 to 11 hours. Any change to road class, vehicle speed, parameter bounds, or damper model requires a complete re-run. For a team iterating through multiple design variants, this becomes a serious bottleneck.
The second approach is the Deep Learning Surrogate model. Generation of data for training the model which could be adams simulation based data or even the sensor based data which gives us a leverage of capturing the near actual dynamics of the system 
Here, we first generate training data from ODE simulations, train a deep learning model to replicate the system's response, and then use that trained model as a fast proxy inside the optimizer. Once trained, the surrogate predicts RMS acceleration in milliseconds — enabling hundreds of optimization iterations at negligible cost.
The trade-off: there is an upfront cost in generating the training data and training the model, and the accuracy is bounded by the quality and quantity of that training data.
These two approaches are not competing — they are complementary, as you'll see.
The physics-based model is built on a system of Ordinary Differential Equations that describe the dynamics of the suspension system — how the sprung and unsprung masses respond to axle inputs over time. 

