Solving 

The ODEs represent a 6-degree-of-freedom suspension model with two geometric constraints — constraints that represent the mechanical coupling between axles in the NRS setup.
To enforce these constraints, we use Lagrange multipliers through a KKT formulation — the Karush-Kuhn-Tucker conditions. These multipliers introduce constraint forces into the equations of motion, ensuring the mathematical solution respects the physical boundaries of the system.
However, numerical integration of constrained differential equations introduces a known problem: constraint drift — where numerical errors accumulate over time and the constraint is gradually violated.
We address this using Baumgarte Stabilization — a technique that adds a correction term to the equations, effectively acting as a critically damped spring that continuously pulls any constraint violation back toward zero. The stabilization parameters control how quickly this correction is applied.
This is what makes the ODE simulation numerically robust over long simulation windows.

