lets move into the optimization which is important for this entire problem The optimization framework supports both single-objective and multi-objective formulations.​
The cabin seat RMS acceleration is computed from three components: bounce acceleration, roll acceleration, and pitch acceleration — all evaluated at the seat level.In single-objective optimization, these three components are aggregated into a single scalar metric, and the optimizer minimizes it directly.​
In multi-objective optimization, each degree of freedom is treated as a separate objective. This produces a Pareto front — a set of optimal trade-off solutions where improving one objective comes at the cost of another. The engineer can then choose the solution that best fits their design priorities
The first is Single-Objective Optimization using Bayesian Optimization. Bayesian Optimization is a probabilistic, global optimization method — it builds a surrogate model, typically a Gaussian Process, and uses an acquisition function to decide where to sample next. It balances exploration — searching new regions of the parameter space — with exploitation — refining the best solutions found so far. The key advantage here is efficiency: it finds good solutions with far fewer function evaluations than traditional methods, which makes it well suited for our computationally expensive ODE simulations. The output is a single optimal solution — one best parameter set that minimizes the aggregated RMS across all degrees of freedom.​
The second is Multi-Objective Optimization using NSGA-II — the Non-dominated Sorting Genetic Algorithm. This is a population-based search method that runs multiple candidate solutions simultaneously, uses non-dominated sorting to identify Pareto-optimal solutions, and maintains solution diversity through crowding distance. Instead of collapsing everything into one number, it optimizes each degree of freedom independently and returns a Pareto front — a set of trade-off solutions where improving one objective comes at the cost of another.​
The practical implication: with single-objective, you get one answer. With NSGA-II, you get a range of optimal configurations and the engineer chooses based on their design priority — whether that’s prioritizing bounce comfort, minimizing pitch, or balancing all three.​
Both methods are embedded in the framework and can be selected based on the problem at hand.​
