Transition​ 
Multi-objective optimization using NSGA-II outperforms single-objective optimization — it gives access to the full trade-off space and lets the engineer make an informed decision rather than accepting a single aggregated answer. That is a meaningful advantage.​
But here is the bottleneck we cannot ignore: 10 to 11 hours per optimization run. Every design variation — different road class, different speed, different parameter bounds — requires a full re-run. For a team working through multiple iterations, this is not viable at scale.​
The physics-based model remains essential for three things: it is the ground truth, it requires no training data, it is fully interpretable, and it is the validation benchmark for any surrogate.​
But to get the speed needed for rapid iteration, we need a surrogate. And to make the surrogate physically reliable, we need it to be constrained by the governing physics — not just fitted to data.​
That is exactly what a Physics-Informed Neural Network gives us. A model trained not just on simulation data, but penalized for violating the equations of motion themselves. The network learns physically consistent solutions even in regions where training data is sparse.​
PINN​
A standard neural network is trained purely to minimize the gap between its predictions and the training data. A Physics-Informed Neural Network does something more: it embeds the governing ODE directly into the training loss.​
The total training loss has two components:​
Data Loss — the mean squared error between predicted and simulated outputs.​
Physics Loss — the squared ODE residual. If the network's output violates the equation of motion, this term penalizes it, regardless of how well it matches the training data.​
The total loss is a weighted combination: L = λ₁ · L_data + λ₂ · L_phys​
This means the network can only learn solutions that are both data-consistent and physically consistent. It cannot overfit noisy data in ways that violate mechanics. This is particularly valuable when training data is limited or noisy — the physics acts as a regularizer.​

